    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Disease and trauma to the mammalian central nervous system (CNS) result in persistent, functional deficits because axons fail to regenerate and the tissue never fully recover. Glial cells maintain the structure and function of the nervous system, but following injury these cells become reactive and undergo significant molecular and functional changes. These phenotypic changes result in the formation of a glial scar, which is composed almost entirely of reactive astrocytes. Oligodendrocytes release myelin debris and microglia release cytokines into the site surrounding the injury, which can determine and perpetuate the subsequent astrogliosis. It is crucial to understand the causes and nature of this astrogliosis and its effect on the regenerating neurons so that potential treatments could reverse this reactivity to facilitate neuron recovery. The present study treats purified astrocytes in vitro with myelin debris, myelin-associated proteins, and the potent cytokine, TNFα and quantifies the subsequent astrogliosis by gene-induction, morphology, motility, and toxicity to other CNS cell types. We found reactivity is heterogeneous and dependent on the specific treatment. TNFα induced high levels of astrogliosis and we found that oligodendrocyte membrane proteins, Nogo molecules, reduce this level of reactivity.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
